Case Study: A Transcript Redaction Service with Flask and Hugging Face
The Scenario
A social-science research lab records interviews with study participants. Before transcripts can be shared with collaborators at other universities, the ethics board (IRB) requires the lab to de-identify them: remove people's names, places, and organizations that could reveal who a participant is.

Today a research assistant does this by hand with find-and-replace. It takes hours per transcript and names get missed. The lab wants a small internal service that does a first pass automatically, so a human only needs to review the result.

The catch: lab members don't all use Python. Some analyze data in R, some work in Excel, and the project coordinator just wants a web page. So the model has to sit behind a simple web API that any tool can call.

Model: dslim/bert-base-NER, a named-entity recognition model on the Hugging Face Hub. It finds persons (PER), locations (LOC), organizations (ORG), and miscellaneous names (MISC) in English text.

Why Flask is the Right Tool Here
This is a textbook Flask project:

One job, one model. The service loads one model and exposes a few endpoints. There are no user accounts, no admin panel, and no database, so Django's built-in ORM, admin, and auth would go unused.
Python where the model lives. Hugging Face models run in Python. Flask wraps them in HTTP with very little code, so R scripts, spreadsheets, and browsers can all use the same model.
Small enough to run anywhere. The whole service fits on a lab workstation or a small server, which matters when data can't leave the university network.
Learning Goals
Wrap a Hugging Face model in a Flask JSON API that non-Python clients can call.
Load a model once and keep it separate from the web code.
Validate input and return clear JSON errors.
Test a Flask app without downloading the model.
Think about what an ML model can and cannot be trusted to do with sensitive data.
What to Build
One web page and two JSON endpoints.

Method

Path

What it does

