/api/health

Returns {"status": "ok", "model": "dslim/bert-base-NER"}

POST

/api/redact

Takes JSON text, returns the redacted text and the entities found

Example request and response:

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/redact \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"text": "I met Dr. Maria Lopez at Riverside Clinic in Denver. Email me at maria@example.com."}'

 

{
  "redacted_text": "I met Dr. [PER] at [ORG] in [LOC]. Email me at [EMAIL].",
  "entities": [
    {"type": "PER", "text": "Maria Lopez", "start": 10, "end": 21, "score": 0.998},
    {"type": "ORG", "text": "Riverside Clinic", "start": 25, "end": 41, "score": 0.975},
    {"type": "LOC", "text": "Denver", "start": 45, "end": 51, "score": 0.999},
    {"type": "EMAIL", "text": "maria@example.com", "start": 65, "end": 82, "score": 1.0}
  ],
  "model": "dslim/bert-base-NER"
}

(Scores are illustrative; your model output will differ slightly.)

Rules:

text is required: a string of 1 to 10,000 characters.
types is optional: a list chosen from PER, LOC, ORG, MISC. If omitted, redact all four. Anything else is an error.
Only redact entities with a score of at least 80. Put this threshold in the app's config, not hard-coded in a function.
Replace each entity with its type in brackets, such as [PER].
The NER model does not detect email addresses, so also replace emails with [EMAIL] using a regular expression. Emails are always redacted, whatever types
Errors return JSON like {"error": "text must be 1 to 10,000 characters"} with status 400. Unknown API URLs return JSON 404s, not HTML pages.
Privacy rule: never save or log the submitted text. This service handles sensitive research data.
How to Structure 
Keep it small, but keep the model separate from the web code.

redaction_service/
├── app.py              # create_app(), routes, error handlers
├── redactor.py         # model loading, entity finding, redaction logic
├── templates/
│   └── index.html
├── tests/
│   └── test_app.py
├── client_example.R    # or .py / .js: a non-browser client (see Deliverables)
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md

Requirements:

py has no Flask in it. It holds a Redactor class with a method such as redact(text, types) -> dict. It is the only file that imports transformers.
Load the model once. Create one Redactor when the app starts, not inside a route. Loading takes several seconds; doing it per request would make every request slow.
Use a small application factory: create_app(redactor=None). If a redactor is passed in, use it; otherwise build the real one. This is what lets your tests swap in a fake.
Routes stay thin. A route reads input, calls the redactor, and returns JSON or a template. Validation errors become 400 responses.
Tests without the model. Write at least 6 pytest tests using a FakeRedactor (or a fake model) that returns fixed entities. Cover a successful redaction, the email rule, the score threshold, invalid types, empty or too-long text, and a JSON 404.
Model tips:

from transformers import pipeline

ner = pipeline("ner", model="dslim/bert-base-NER", aggregation_strategy="simple")
ner("Maria Lopez works at Riverside Clinic.")
# [{'entity_group': 'PER', 'score': 0.998, 'word': 'Maria Lopez', 'start': 0, 'end': 11}, ...]

aggregation_strategy="simple" merges word pieces ("Mar", "##ia") into whole names. Without it you get fragments.
Each result has start and end character positions. Replace entities from the end of the text backwards, so earlier positions stay valid as the text changes length.
score comes back as a NumPy number; convert it with float() before returning JSON.
The model downloads (about 400 MB) the first time. Tests must not trigger this.
